{"title":"1989 IMO Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A permutation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/0/d3035ec8b76095a1a897222e4d4ca371e6df5330.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{x_1, \\ldots, x_{2n}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" /> of the set<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/7/427971afb2d5aabd3e2ed3bca718ffa26aadc29e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2, \\ldots, 2n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/e/85e1dcdaf9c52cc0674b41edc21f8fd74a4191d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mbox{n}$\" width=\"9\" height=\"7\" /> is a positive integer, is said to have property <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/c/9acb77bb66fccb38918c95b67f0a99bb3c86e2f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x_i - x_{i + 1}| = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" /> for at least one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/3/9c3b493b93f5c7f199cfcad8175015b13352bcc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i \\in \\{1,2, \\ldots, 2n - 1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"163\" height=\"18\" />. Show that, for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/e/85e1dcdaf9c52cc0674b41edc21f8fd74a4191d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mbox{n}$\" width=\"9\" height=\"7\" />, there are more permutations with property <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> than without.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>So for the specific case when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>We have the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/1/ed123aec81a19e7c53d2af259f3b30d52b9b6d5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3,4\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>To satisfy the condition, the 2 numbers must be adjacent and we can have either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/0/fa071cf8bae698b56fdf3ff330ec7cfed0663c71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,3), (3,1), (2,4), (4,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"184\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> represents an adjacent pair.\n</p><p>To find those that satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> we need to find: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/2/322e8425f9d9f6cc6715108d3d4e85d35e1f853e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,3) \\cup (3,1) \\cup (2,4) \\cup (4,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"220\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Using PIE we can find those that doesn't satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/2/3229950d0e7943312ea7effdd474c5450e5abacb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{(1,3) \\cup (3,1) \\cup (2,4) \\cup (4,2)} = \\overline{(1,3)} \\cap \\overline{(3,1)} \\cap \\overline{(2,4)} \\cap \\overline{(4,2)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"469\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/0/740862128ec9863fe2f0183c304bd4f7f4c75911.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q = |\\overline{(1,3)} \\cap \\overline{(3,1)} \\cap \\overline{(2,4)} \\cap \\overline{(4,2)}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"269\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Defining an indicator function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/f/18f6728900a2dc37575e01953483ad0b005e972a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_y(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/b/fdb4f320cae29c149f027114623b8544b2b1f095.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\in \\{(1,3), (3,1), (2,4), (4,2)\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"234\" height=\"18\" /> with domain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/8/428d4a99cf1884a7ec791d7f0427d6c5d8ceaf9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\mathbb{U}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/d/badcd40637464043e162d44c7637163c29e814e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{U}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> contains all sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/9/2b95e7663a6a12ebb60e0f330b82a6a903f95cac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_y(x) = \\begin{cases}  0,  &amp; \\mbox{if} \\ \\mbox{x} \\ \\notin \\ \\mbox{y} \\\\  1, &amp; \\mbox{if} \\ \\mbox{x} \\ \\in \\ \\mbox{y} \\end{cases}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"175\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/5/a95a611858d5681027cfd413fad692d6b7e73e57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q = \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} I_{\\overline{(1,3)}}(x) I_{\\overline{(3,1)}}(x)I_{\\overline{(2,4)}}(x)I_{\\overline{(4,2)}}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"313\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/3/67373225b278b05a972ffe961904d1eeb9598dce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q = \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} (1-I_{(1,3)}(x))(1-I_{(3,1)}(x))(1-I_{(2,4)}(x))(1-I_{(4,2)}(x))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"488\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/4/79438f6f01251c5f962edb6d72737179f8a70f0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q = \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} 1 - \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}}I_{(1,3)}(x)+I_{(3,1)}(x)+I_{(2,4)}(x)+I_{(4,2)}(x)+\\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(3,1)}(x) + I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(2,4)}(x)+I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(4,2)}(x) + I_{(3,1)}(x)I_{(2,4)}(x) +I_{(3,1)}(x)I_{(4,2)}(x)+I_{(2,4)}(x)I_{(4,2)}(x)-\\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}}I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(3,1)}(x)I_{(2,4)}(x)+I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(2,4)}(x)I_{(4,2)}(x)+I_{(3,1)}(x)I_{(2,4)}(x)I_{(4,2)}(x)+I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(3,1)}(x)I_{(4,2)}(x) + \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}}I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(3,1)}(x)I_{(2,4)}(x)I_{(4,2)}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"1049\" height=\"128\" />\n</p><p>Now to work out the cardinality of each consider the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/d/4edf5fb380e0f9ce8c6f0ea39b4955b0ca5ce876.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{(1,3), (3,1)\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/2/ec23d93bef26edef3deb74a7c9a02a12bcf94db3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{(2,4), (4,2)\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The first sum is obvious: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/c/e1c1156619216c63150e86c9e54ef0b9ab5dfbd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} 1 = 4!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"74\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>The second sum is also pretty obvious: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/3/11308e0ae5e52ca96cb33d8077c0127b1287e70e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}}I_{(1,3)}(x)+I_{(3,1)}(x)+I_{(2,4)}(x)+I_{(4,2)}(x) = 4!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"376\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>The third sum is not so obvious since we have terms that equal 0, eg, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c76a496c6bae7bb52ce11ebd18a55c0a05cd6e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_{(1,3)}(x)I_{(3,1)}(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"154\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>Thus we need to pick any 2 pairs from the 1st set and any 2 pairs from the 2nd set.\n</p><p>So there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/a/4aae69d5a3cf3e7239ddd216b41562e0c55d903f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> non-zero pairs. Each pair however has 2 ways to rearrange. So the third sum equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/c/65c6fca75f0c549fa301f6b5ee1fa7642ec6cc90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 4 =8$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"73\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>The fourth sum is 0 since we need 3 sets but we only have 2 to pick from.\n</p><p>The fifth sum is also 0 by the same argument as above.\n</p><p>Now we can generalise.\n</p><p>Consider the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/d/06d1a0ac994a00357df5dfcfbd0740570e75f77d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3, \\cdots, 2n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/0/3b08507f2a9ccff6527c01227e52ea268fe92d20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1+n), (1+n,1), (2,2+n), (2+n,2), \\cdots, (n,2n),(2n,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"474\" height=\"18\" /> represent the adjacent pairs. There are a total of 2n pairs.\n</p><p>To find those that satisfy T we need to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/2/67267c4ed80b714708953ae38acf77a61f166575.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1+n) \\cup (1+n,1) \\cup (2,2+n) \\cup (2+n,2) \\cup \\cdots \\cup (n,2n) \\cup (2n,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"541\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Using PIE we can find the complement of T: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/a/e6a22aa7f193045234b2338c236f7f158c63531c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{(1,1+n) \\cup (1+n,1) \\cup (2,2+n) \\cup (2+n,2) \\cup \\cdots \\cup (n,2n) \\cup (2n,n)} = \\overline{(1,1+n)} \\cap \\overline{(1+n,1)} \\cap \\overline{(2,2+n)} \\cap \\overline{(2+n,2)} \\cap \\cdots \\cap \\overline{(n,2n)} \\cap \\overline{(2n,n)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"1049\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96a363a049a50f27ee7743f210b315b7623a9a0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n = |\\overline{(1,1+n)} \\cap \\overline{(1+n,1)} \\cap \\overline{(2,2+n)} \\cap \\overline{(2+n,2)} \\cap \\cdots \\cap \\overline{(n,2n)} \\cap \\overline{(2n,n)}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"598\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Now we define an indicator function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/f/18f6728900a2dc37575e01953483ad0b005e972a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_y(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/f/baf54fcc4829d5c5388c40ceef94b845be1b3091.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\in \\{(1,1+n), (1+n,1), (2,2+n), (2+n,2), \\cdots, (n,2n),(2n,n)\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"525\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/9/2b95e7663a6a12ebb60e0f330b82a6a903f95cac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_y(x) = \\begin{cases}  0,  &amp; \\mbox{if} \\ \\mbox{x} \\ \\notin \\ \\mbox{y} \\\\  1, &amp; \\mbox{if} \\ \\mbox{x} \\ \\in \\ \\mbox{y} \\end{cases}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"175\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/2/cd2e85462c6c0d0e604b70e23089ebefff625749.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n = \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} (1-I_{(1,1+n)}(x))(1-I_{(1+n,1)}(x))(1-I_{(2,2+n)}(x))(1-I_{(2+n,2)}(x)) \\cdots (1-I_{(n,2n)}(x))(1-I_{(2n,n)}(x))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"830\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/6/8561b603851d51397448d6b5e2eb784393baa5f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n = \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} 1 - \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} I_{(1,1+n)}(x)+I_{(1+n,1)}(x)+ \\cdots + I_{(n,2n)}(x) + I_{(2n,n)}(x) + \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} I_{(1,1+n)}(x)I_{(1+n,1)}(x)+I_{(1,1+n)}(x)I_{(2,2+n)}(x)+ \\cdots + I_{(1+n,n)}(x)I_{(2n,n)}(x) \\cdots - \\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}}I_{(1,1+n)}(x)I_{(1+n,1)}(x)I_{(2,2+n)}(x) \\cdots+\\sum_{x \\in \\mathbb{U}} (I_{(1,1+n)}(x))(I_{(1+n,1)}(x))(I_{(2,2+n)}(x))(I_{(2+n,2)}(x)) \\cdots (I_{(n,2n)}(x))(I_{(2n,n)}(x))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"1106\" height=\"84\" />\n</p><p>The first sum equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/c/e7c65c2a874b19bdb25e07737a8a098ccd4c292e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The second sum equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30bd28ac4283f2c3d387cdfd697afa7442298c7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{2n}{1}(2n-1)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"117\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>The third sum is a bit tricky since some pairs equal 0, thus consider all the different pairs placed into sets like this:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/5/0e59ae65558425405a7de633b8f004712404a9c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{(1,1+n), (1+n,1)\\}, \\{(2,2+n), (2+n,2)\\}, \\{(3,3+n), (3+n,n)\\}, \\cdots, \\{(n,2n), (2n,n)\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"713\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We need 2 pairs, since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/e/85e1dcdaf9c52cc0674b41edc21f8fd74a4191d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mbox{n}$\" width=\"9\" height=\"7\" /> sets, we need to pick 2 sets first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/e/f3ea58310f52fd0a85cbaae0858295612fe90135.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\implies \\binom{n}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"79\" height=\"53\" />. But each set contains 2 terms, thus we can have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/a/32a35631e1e126934ead6d2af161e9d250827bbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> different pairings for each 2 sets.\n</p><p>Therefore this sum equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/d/4edbba04d53319dc5fb4fb99c5d1cc3b3c805999.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2\\binom{n}{2}(2n-2)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"124\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>The fourth sum is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/6/8e606c315a289b576b51a41828ace578279f19e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^3\\binom{n}{3}(2n-3)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"124\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>The fifth sum is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/4/6b4cbea0901ab7227a1dbcece2ff7a74b2d01d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^4\\binom{n}{4}(2n-4)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"124\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>.\n.\n.\n</p><p>The last sum is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/7/407c38a3d506f6f4e133f8fdf86a028181f7e186.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2n}\\binom{n}{2n}(2n-2n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"152\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>In total we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/2/af2df2d6a5228dd61099da8f9b7e035544e5ec6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n = (2n)!-(2n)! + 2^2\\binom{n}{2}(2n-2)! - 2^3\\binom{n}{3}(2n-3)! + 2^4\\binom{n}{4}(2n-4)! - \\cdots + 2^{2n}\\binom{n}{2n}(2n-2n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"815\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/d/3edca613dedacc49bd7e8f4797a3832d44c8d169.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n = \\sum_{i=2}^{n}(-1)^{i}2^i\\binom{n}{i}(2n-i)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"236\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>So that means there are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/9/d29e04bbd77de0b9795acf7763c8fa9a8182806c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" /> sets which does not satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Now we just have to prove that the number of sets that satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is larger than those that don't.\n</p><p>The number of sets that satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/125e8a103ca1cdf2ef94706667efb83e3c0b3e01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n)!-Q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>So we need to prove <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/2/0d20c7384ab682a637d72badda7cef2a60e851c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n)!-Q_n &gt; Q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/9/2c9f6b4fb3fe0e018be4be1252b2915967f243ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\implies (2n)! &gt; 2Q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>First let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/3/273e0046b505d99f3d1167caa82b2ac76c96d075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> represent <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/5/d65a2d75df8ec54b51d73ba40b9a3c82e09320e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|(-1)^{i}2^i\\binom{n}{i}(2n-i)!|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"172\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/9/6c970107ea69811e4ccaeb86bb1bc2edbf4fd201.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\therefore Q_n = t_2-t_3+t_4-t_5+ \\cdots -t_{2n-1} + t_{2n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"335\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>We see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/f/6bfdacb1c6511386774c76c3c6c0e7f483403a35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2t_2 = (-1)^{2}2^3\\binom{n}{2}(2n-2)! = (2^2)\\left(\\frac{n!}{(n-2)!2!}\\right)(2n-2)! = (2^3)\\left(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\right)(2n-2)! = 2n(2n-2)(2n-2)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"863\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/a/7aae4430d0f2f502ad95bad5916d566cbfd793b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n)! = (2n)(2n-1)(2n-2)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"232\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/f/b6f41d02bf7c3020211b8dbfd689122e4af4c22e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n)(2n-1)(2n-2)! &gt; 2n(2n-2)(2n-2)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"348\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/2/e728714bb5a56a742300c25229bbad270e0265b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\implies (2n)! &gt; 2t_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Next take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/1/e51a3ca6ab05c0125032af6cc66844243477601d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/5/0852123751116adab4f7e1723d094f9341fbebfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"14\" /> for example.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/1/e51a3ca6ab05c0125032af6cc66844243477601d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> means at least 3 pairs satisfy T and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/5/0852123751116adab4f7e1723d094f9341fbebfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"14\" /> means at least 4 pairs satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>But at least 4 pairs is a subset of at least 3 pairs which means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/9/169bf32ad35864c6dce9fb847c3362bcbcba47ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_4 \\subset t_3 \\implies |t_3| \\ge |t_4|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"172\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Generalising this leads to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/6/606cbef3e029af607723c412fdafc3cebb637c1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_i \\ge t_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79084c3cc8faff2d7eb255775da002596df1272e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2Q_n = 2t_2 + 2(t_4-t_3) + \\cdots 2(t_{2n} - t_{2n-1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"327\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/3/03347040457c47abfac045ecb508261c43df4fdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{(t_4-t_3), \\cdots, (t_{2n}-t_{2n-1})\\} \\le 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"256\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b93c39ef6b1ee166e448e07edebf54533b47af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\implies 2t_2 + 2(t_4-t_3) + \\cdots 2(t_{2n} - t_{2n-1}) \\le 2t_2 &lt; (2n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"425\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/7/bf79a7fd5dcd059c666e79bc63a48e23d825168f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\therefore 2Q_n &lt; (2n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/8/bb8fe7d350872f5d4f5edaad1112139b96209782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mbox{Q.E.D}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of permutations with property <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/e/c2ececdc0bb3ce97a20ed0b4fb7c3b1dd8383e9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> be the set of permutations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/c/b0c751113a4ee1822e1d774b61fd9986cf2f9bc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{ x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{2n} \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" />  such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/c/e0cd86ca76e8e88c1376d62d79c8233f9c56abda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x_i - x_{i+1}| = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" />. By the inclusion-exclusion principle,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/f/d6fb0391b4a843b182c6e5c97a4f7e2279b06a2c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|F| = \\bigg|\\bigcup_{i=1}^n F_i \\bigg| = \\sum_{i=1}^{2n-1} |F_i| - \\sum_{1\\leq i&lt;j \\leq 2n-1} |F_i \\cap F_j| + E \\tag{1}\\]\" width=\"447\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/0/c20c23371a9c0d87e576b54743dd17fbe46a543a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"47\" height=\"15\" />. Let’s calculate the first two sums on the far right.\n</p><p>For any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/c/a5c65938d64e3832879467f0dfa687d0ff1dec4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|F_i| = 2n \\cdot (2n - 2)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"157\" height=\"20\" />, since there are are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> choices for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67bc6daa9d6b964201d6cef60cbeb1ac5fd26ead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" />, which fixes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/c/b5c170dc53fce7452e6f7df461222672d913e34e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/f/48f7cf7fdc00dc32cde6df9348ccf0301170f8aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n-2)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> choices for the remaining elements. Thus\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/9/b79984d56c90735102ede723b1a090c776fe143f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{i=1}^{2n - 1}|F_i| = 2n \\cdot (2n - 2)! \\cdot (2n - 1) = (2n)!\\]\" width=\"330\" height=\"50\" />\n</p><p>Now let’s compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/4/624556e0358413155a779f0ff2a9356dc2b1cc05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|F_i \\cap F_j|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"68\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/7/ef766a1bcbda2977468173c803dd768da56cd543.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\leq i &lt; j \\leq 2n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"150\" height=\"17\" />. It’s not possible that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/5/9d5f0c0cacf64e2bc3b681dffdec2ba38e957746.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x_i - x_{i+1}| = |x_{i+1}- x_{i+2}| = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"240\" height=\"20\" />, since that would imply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/e/4ae6c523a1bee645da85701e0ade780c04005706.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i = x_{i+2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"14\" />. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/5/245d78f38888eda5d4bb5064ea1cbd94da921c5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|F_i \\cap F_j| = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/3/7d3fb2e248caab3910891d1a838bce73fe080628.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j = i + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"70\" height=\"16\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/9/28957289aace21f071d5cf1f758f3399ce766748.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j &gt; i + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"70\" height=\"16\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/f/9efe88f51f52e970da2e22f8a76c97afa51a55fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|F_i \\cap F_j| = 2n \\cdot (2n - 2) \\cdot (2n - 4)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"275\" height=\"20\" /> (there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> choices for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/b/15bc2195a401ef8f01f2b1ae68c369400b9b2211.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i, x_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/4/3049a5f0880fe9aa64ca27aea271fa068407ff56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n - 2$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/7/127f9161309db767ee5e31338a37495f9c177d13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_j, x_{j+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/6/3e6af164fba8f4e6de643698686df7a21b104cc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n - 4)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"72\" height=\"20\" /> for everything else.)\n</p><p>How often is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/6/616c549557475a9427f108355296099677327af3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|F_i \\cap F_j| \\neq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" />? We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/7/eb7d7b7183a426af11357550370d7ede2aee4b61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n - 3$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" />, and for any value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/c/eec0d9373d10b33eec652337a9685107d7de4b79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n - 2 - i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"82\" height=\"15\" /> possible values for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" />. So the number of non-empty intersections <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/4/624556e0358413155a779f0ff2a9356dc2b1cc05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|F_i \\cap F_j|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"68\" height=\"20\" /> is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/0/d60edb1e6600110bdb3eede663158c75ae2fcf5a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{i=1}^{2n-3} 2n - 2 - i = \\sum_{i=1}^{2n-3}i = \\frac{(2n-3)(2n-2)}{2}.\\]\" width=\"342\" height=\"50\" />\nNow we can compute\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/b/a1b414e37d338f0363524fe938d8c0fa1ed576ef.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\sum_{1\\leq i &lt; j \\leq 2n-1} |F_i \\cap F_j| &amp;= 2n \\cdot (2n - 2) \\cdot (2n - 4)! \\cdot \\frac{(2n - 3)(2n - 2)}{2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{2n \\cdot (2n-2)^2 \\cdot (2n - 3)!}{2} = \\frac{(2n)!}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2n - 2}{2n - 1} \\\\ &amp;&lt; \\frac{(2n)!}{2}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"501\" height=\"142\" />\n</p><p>Let’s plug this into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/9/0d9c3b1c142cbd3f8217dabc160c57e4750df241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/3/6730ac71f23f16a3b649077be14fb81ccbef8d9e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|F| = \\sum_{i=1}^{2n - 1} |F_i| - \\sum_{1\\leq i &lt; j \\leq 2n-1} |F_i \\cap F_j| + E &gt; (2n)! - \\frac{(2n)!}{2} + E \\geq \\frac{(2n)!}{2}.\\]\" width=\"513\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>So more than half of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/c/e7c65c2a874b19bdb25e07737a8a098ccd4c292e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/f/56f3fa273645372fdaa189053fc6bdb6c1c3082b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, \\ldots, 2n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" /> have property <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1eccfbd65334353f671fb71c27b9b9f2c689b0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> be the set of permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/a/bcac7e30a53c07f5b9865341d9ad26f647db0f8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2, \\ldots,2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" /> not having property <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9be2b1de79fff66db67de3b18303bdd2912b7ae6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" /> be the set of permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/a/bcac7e30a53c07f5b9865341d9ad26f647db0f8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2, \\ldots,2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" /> having exactly one value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/0/37051069390ac25b86d09ce4be1d08d3dcec64e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\geq2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/5/315eee3b7457db32f4b9c7f741bc9700b99e52e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x_k-x_{k+1}|=n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"123\" height=\"20\" />. We will prove a 1-1 correspondence between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1eccfbd65334353f671fb71c27b9b9f2c689b0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9be2b1de79fff66db67de3b18303bdd2912b7ae6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nConsider any permutation in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1eccfbd65334353f671fb71c27b9b9f2c689b0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" />. Now just take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/ccada11db7b2b90693e2fac4f887a57fce6f96bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and for the unique value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/9/dc910b7bf02ebae850be90384348914d8bcfbd4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x_1-x_k|=n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"20\" />, put <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/ccada11db7b2b90693e2fac4f887a57fce6f96bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> in the spot right before <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />. This will give us a permutation that belongs in the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9be2b1de79fff66db67de3b18303bdd2912b7ae6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" />. Now consider a permutation in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9be2b1de79fff66db67de3b18303bdd2912b7ae6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" />. If you take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d3dead9cb8d5f6327c737e014850be7a29d0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> and put it as the first term, you'll get a permutation that belongs in set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1eccfbd65334353f671fb71c27b9b9f2c689b0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" />.Therefore, we've proved a 1-1 correspondence between the two.\n</p><p><br />\nBecause <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/9/4292b8ad70eff264667e771835160c6051779593.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_n|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"20\" /> is clearly less than the number of permutations with property <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, we finally have that the number of permutations with property <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is greater than the number of permutations without <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n~BennettHuang\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO\" title=\"1989 IMO\">1989 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1989 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1989 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1989 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1989 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1989 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1989 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1989_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1989 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Question</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017181645\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.053 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.065 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 719/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 867/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 51/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 29770/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.955      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.955      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:8428:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017181645 and revision id 259383. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}